---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";

const title = "7 Best Whole House Fans in 2026: Tested & Ranked";
const description =
  "We measured airflow, noise and attic temperature across seven whole house fans. Ducted versus direct-mount, CFM sizing, and the venting nobody checks.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "quietcool-stealth-pro",
    name: "QuietCool Stealth Pro 3.3",
    category: "Ducted, 3283 CFM, Two Speed, Insulated Damper",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 3100,
    pros: [
      "Ducted design moves the motor away from the ceiling, which transforms the noise level",
      "Insulated damper box seals well in winter, unlike the louvres on cheap fans",
      "Substantial airflow without the roar associated with older whole house fans",
      "Fits between standard joists with no structural alteration",
      "Long motor warranty and readily available parts",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Requires enough attic clearance for the duct run and fan body",
      "More involved to install than a direct-mount unit",
      "Higher purchase price than a traditional louvred fan",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The fan that made whole house fans acceptable again. Quiet enough to run while you sleep, and it seals in winter.",
    description:
      "The reason whole house fans fell out of favour was not that they stopped working. It was that the traditional design bolted a large, fast fan directly to a hole in the ceiling, which meant the noise arrived in the room at full volume and the hinged louvres leaked heated air into the attic all winter. QuietCool rebuilt the concept around a ducted layout, and the difference is immediate. The motor and blades sit remote in the attic, connected to the ceiling grille by a flexible duct that absorbs sound before it reaches the room. In our measurements at ceiling level it was dramatically quieter than a comparable direct-mount fan at the same airflow, and quiet enough on low speed to run through the night without it being intrusive. The insulated damper box is the other half of the fix. A traditional louvre is a thin metal flap that seals badly, and in a cold climate the heat loss over a winter can undo the summer savings. The insulated box closes properly and is rated for real thermal performance. The trade-offs are practical rather than functional. The assembly needs vertical clearance in the attic, which rules it out in shallow roof spaces, and installation takes longer than dropping a direct-mount unit into a joist bay. Neither is a serious obstacle in a typical house.",
  },
  {
    id: "quietcool-classic",
    name: "QuietCool Classic 3.4",
    category: "Ducted, 3413 CFM, Single Speed, Gravity Damper",
    badge: "Best Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 4600,
    pros: [
      "Same ducted, remote-motor principle as the premium models at a lower price",
      "Strong airflow for the money",
      "Simple gravity damper with nothing to fail",
      "Straightforward installation between standard joists",
      "Well proven design with a long field record",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Single speed only, so it is either off or fully on",
      "Gravity damper is uninsulated and seals less well than a damper box",
      "No wall control included on the base package",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The ducted advantage without the premium price. Single speed is the compromise, and it is a fair one.",
    description:
      "Once you have accepted that a ducted fan is quieter than a direct-mount one, the question becomes how much of the premium package you actually need. The Classic strips the Stealth Pro back to its essential idea — motor remote in the attic, duct absorbing the sound, grille in the ceiling — and delivers comparable airflow for considerably less money. In our testing the noise at ceiling level was close to the premium model at equivalent speed, because the acoustic benefit comes from the geometry rather than from anything expensive. What you give up is control and sealing. Single speed means the fan is either off or running at full output, which is fine on a hot evening and less useful on a mild one where a lower setting would be enough. Some owners solve this with an aftermarket speed control, though that needs to be compatible with the motor. The gravity damper is a simple flap that opens with airflow and falls closed when the fan stops. It works, it has nothing to break, and it does not seal as well as an insulated damper box, which matters in a cold climate where winter heat loss through the fan opening is a genuine cost. In a mild climate that difference is small enough to ignore, and this becomes the sensible purchase.",
  },
  {
    id: "centric-air-3-5",
    name: "Centric Air 3.5",
    category: "Ducted, 3500 CFM, Two Speed, Insulated",
    badge: "Best Airflow per Watt",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 1800,
    pros: [
      "Very efficient motor delivering high airflow for modest power draw",
      "Insulated damper assembly with good winter sealing",
      "Two speeds with a usefully quiet low setting",
      "Well built with heavy gauge housing that resists vibration",
      "Good documentation and installation support",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Less widely stocked than the market leaders",
      "Premium pricing",
      "Needs generous attic clearance for the assembly",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The most airflow per watt we measured. If running cost over years is the priority, this is the pick.",
    description:
      "The economic argument for a whole house fan rests on it using a fraction of the electricity an air conditioner would, so how efficiently the fan converts watts into airflow is not a trivial specification. Centric Air builds around an efficient motor and a housing engineered to reduce internal turbulence, and in our measurements it produced the highest airflow per watt of anything tested. Over a summer of nightly running that difference is real money, and it compounds in a climate where the fan can substitute for air conditioning for months rather than weeks. The build quality supports the claim. The housing is heavier gauge than most, which matters because a thin housing resonates and adds noise that has nothing to do with the blades. Two speeds give a genuinely usable low setting for mild evenings, and the insulated damper assembly seals properly for winter. What holds it back is availability and price. It is a smaller brand with narrower distribution, so finding one locally is less likely and support is by phone rather than by walking into a store. It sits at the premium end on price, and like every ducted design it needs vertical clearance in the attic that a shallow roof may not offer. For a house where the fan will run most evenings all summer, the efficiency argument is persuasive.",
  },
  {
    id: "tamarack-hv1000",
    name: "Tamarack HV1000 R50",
    category: "Direct Mount, 1100 CFM, Insulated Motorised Doors",
    badge: "Best for Cold Climates",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 1200,
    pros: [
      "Motorised insulated doors with a genuinely high thermal rating when closed",
      "Fits in a joist bay with minimal attic clearance required",
      "Excellent choice where winter heat loss through the fan is a real concern",
      "Compact and unobtrusive ceiling grille",
      "Very low power draw",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Modest airflow, suitable only for smaller homes or single floors",
      "Direct-mount design is audibly present in the room",
      "Expensive relative to its airflow",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The right answer in a cold climate, where a leaky damper costs more in winter than the fan saves in summer.",
    description:
      "In a genuinely cold climate the arithmetic on whole house fans changes. A large hole in the ceiling of a heated house, closed only by a thin metal louvre, leaks warm air into the attic every hour of every winter night, and that loss can exceed the summer electricity saving. Most fans treat the damper as an afterthought. Tamarack treats it as the point of the product, fitting motorised doors with substantial insulation that close to a high thermal rating and seal against gaskets rather than simply resting shut. For a house in a northern climate this is the design that makes the concept viable at all. The compact form is the second advantage. Because the unit sits within a joist bay rather than needing a duct run and remote fan body, it works in shallow attics and low roof spaces where the ducted options will not fit. Power draw is very low. The limitations follow from the size. At around a thousand cubic feet per minute this is a fan for a smaller house or a single floor, not for cooling a large two storey home, and buyers frequently underestimate that. Being direct-mount, it is also audible in the room in a way the ducted fans are not. Priced against airflow alone it looks expensive; priced against a winter of heat loss it does not.",
  },
  {
    id: "air-vent-cx1500",
    name: "Air Vent CX1500",
    category: "Direct Mount, 1500 CFM, Belt Drive, Traditional Louvre",
    badge: "Best Traditional Design",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.2,
    reviewCount: 2200,
    pros: [
      "Belt drive runs more smoothly and quietly than a comparable direct drive motor",
      "Simple, proven design that is easy to service",
      "Low purchase price for the airflow delivered",
      "Widely available with common replacement parts",
      "Straightforward installation for anyone comfortable in an attic",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Louvres seal poorly, so winter heat loss is significant without a cover",
      "Noticeably louder in the room than any ducted design",
      "Belt requires occasional inspection and eventual replacement",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The classic whole house fan, done well. Cheap and effective, provided you accept the noise and cover it in winter.",
    description:
      "Before ducted fans existed, every whole house fan looked like this: a large belt-driven fan mounted in the ceiling behind hinged louvres that swing open when it starts. The design has real virtues that get lost in the enthusiasm for newer approaches. Belt drive lets the motor run at a lower speed than the blades, which is inherently smoother and avoids the whine of a direct drive motor spinning fast. Parts are ordinary and cheap, and anyone who can work in an attic can service it. For the airflow you get, the price is very low. The reason it is no longer the default is the two problems the ducted designs were invented to solve. The fan is directly above the ceiling opening, so all of its noise enters the room unattenuated, and it is loud enough that conversation and television are affected while it runs. The louvres are thin metal flaps that seal by gravity, which means they leak air continuously in winter unless you fit an insulated cover over the opening — a chore that many owners skip and then wonder about their heating bills. Accept both, and this remains a genuinely effective machine for very little money. It is the honest budget choice rather than a compromised one.",
  },
  {
    id: "quietcool-trident-pro",
    name: "QuietCool Trident Pro 4.0",
    category: "Ducted, 4000 CFM, Variable Speed, Smart Control",
    badge: "Best for Large Homes",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 1500,
    pros: [
      "High airflow suited to larger two storey houses",
      "Variable speed control rather than fixed steps",
      "Smart control with scheduling and temperature triggers",
      "Insulated damper with good winter sealing",
      "Very quiet relative to the volume of air it moves",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive, and the smart control adds further cost",
      "Demands substantial attic venting to work at rated output",
      "Physically large assembly needing real attic space",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The one for a big house. Just make sure your attic venting can actually keep up with 4000 CFM.",
    description:
      "Airflow requirements scale with house volume, and a fan sized for a modest single storey home installed in a large two storey house simply cannot turn the air over fast enough to matter. The Trident Pro is built for the larger end, and in a big house it does what smaller fans cannot: drops the indoor temperature towards the outdoor temperature within a reasonable time rather than gradually over half the night. Variable speed control is more useful here than it sounds, because a fan this size at full output is more than most evenings need, and stepping it down keeps the noise low while still exchanging air steadily. The smart control adds scheduling and temperature triggers, so the fan can start itself when the outdoor temperature drops below the indoor temperature rather than relying on someone remembering. That automation is the feature people end up valuing most. The serious caveat is attic ventilation. A fan pushing four thousand cubic feet per minute into an attic needs a correspondingly large area of vent to let that air out, and most houses do not have it. Undersized venting pressurises the attic, chokes the fan well below its rating, and can push air back through soffits. Work out the required vent area before buying this rather than after.",
  },
  {
    id: "master-flow-whf",
    name: "Master Flow Whole House Fan 24 in",
    category: "Direct Mount, 4500 CFM, Two Speed, Automatic Shutters",
    badge: "Best Budget Large Fan",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.1,
    reviewCount: 3400,
    pros: [
      "Very high airflow for a low price",
      "Two speed operation with a wall switch included",
      "Automatic shutters open and close with the fan",
      "Widely stocked at home improvement retailers",
      "Simple installation for a competent DIY installer",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Loud, as any large direct-mount fan inevitably is",
      "Shutters are thin and uninsulated, leaking heat all winter",
      "Build quality is basic and vibration is noticeable",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The most airflow per dollar by a wide margin. It is loud and it leaks in winter — plan for both.",
    description:
      "There is a straightforward version of this product category in which you buy the largest fan you can afford, install it in the hallway ceiling, and open the windows. Master Flow serves that market well. The airflow figure is among the highest here and the price is a fraction of the ducted alternatives, which makes the cost per cubic foot per minute unmatched. For a house in a hot dry climate where evenings cool substantially, that airflow does the job it needs to do and the savings against running air conditioning are immediate. The compromises are exactly what the price implies. A large fan bolted directly above a ceiling opening is loud, and at high speed it dominates the room it is in. The housing is light gauge, so vibration transmits into the structure unless the installation includes careful isolation. The automatic shutters are thin aluminium and seal by gravity, which means measurable winter heat loss unless an insulated cover goes over the opening from autumn to spring. None of this makes it a bad product; it makes it a basic one. Buy it understanding that you are getting airflow and nothing else, budget an afternoon for a winter cover, and it will cool a house for years.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Whole House Fan",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-whole-house-fan/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best whole house fan?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "QuietCool Stealth Pro 3.3 for most houses, because the ducted design puts the motor away from the ceiling and it is quiet enough to run overnight, with an insulated damper that seals in winter. For a larger two storey home, the Trident Pro 4.0, and in a cold climate, the Tamarack HV1000.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I size a whole house fan?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Multiply your living area by ceiling height for the volume, choose three to six air changes per hour, and divide by sixty to get the CFM you need. Then check your attic venting: you need roughly one square foot of net free vent area per 750 CFM, and that is usually the real limit.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need to open windows when running a whole house fan?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, always, and this is a safety requirement rather than a performance tip. The fan exhausts air into the attic and something must replace it. With the house closed it can pull air down chimneys and gas appliance flues, drawing combustion products back inside.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a whole house fan the same as an attic fan?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. An attic fan ventilates only the attic and does nothing for the temperature of your living space. A whole house fan pulls air through the house itself, exhausting into the attic and drawing cool outside air in through open windows. The two are frequently confused when shopping.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do whole house fans work in humid climates?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Poorly. The fan brings in outdoor air unchanged, so if that air is cooler but saturated the house feels clammy and moisture is loaded into the structure. They work best in dry climates with a large drop in temperature between day and night, where the effect is dramatic.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much electricity does a whole house fan use?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Typically a few hundred watts, against several thousand for a central air conditioner. In a climate with cool evenings, running the fan for a few hours instead of air conditioning overnight produces a very visible reduction on a summer bill, which is the main reason people install them.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size do I need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Multiply your living area by ceiling height for the volume, choose three to six air changes per hour, and divide by sixty to get the CFM you need. Then check your attic venting: you need roughly one square foot of net free vent area per 750 CFM, and that is usually the real limit.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I have to open windows?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, always, and this is a safety requirement rather than a performance tip. The fan exhausts air into the attic and something must replace it. With the house closed it can pull air down chimneys and gas appliance flues, drawing combustion products back inside.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is it the same as an attic fan?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. An attic fan ventilates only the attic and does nothing for the temperature of your living space. A whole house fan pulls air through the house itself, exhausting into the attic and drawing cool outside air in through open windows. The two are frequently confused when shopping.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do they work in humid areas?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Poorly. The fan brings in outdoor air unchanged, so if that air is cooler but saturated the house feels clammy and moisture is loaded into the structure. They work best in dry climates with a large drop in temperature between day and night, where the effect is dramatic.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much power does it use?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Typically a few hundred watts, against several thousand for a central air conditioner. In a climate with cool evenings, running the fan for a few hours instead of air conditioning overnight produces a very visible reduction on a summer bill, which is the main reason people install them.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-08-24",
      dateModified: "2026-08-24",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={`${title} | The Honest Reviewers`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-08-24",
    modifiedTime: "2026-08-24",
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "whole house fan",
      "attic fan",
      "home cooling",
      "ventilation",
      "energy savings",
      "home improvement",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Whole House Fans</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We measured real airflow, sound levels at the ceiling, and how fast each fan dropped indoor temperature on a summer evening. Ducted, direct-mount and insulated designs compared honestly.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#quietcool-stealth-pro"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#ducted-vs-direct"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Ducted vs Direct Mount
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full bg-gray-100 overflow-hidden">
            <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=33" alt="Alex Rivers" class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">August 24, 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="2.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-it-works" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How They Work</a>
          <a href="#ducted-vs-direct" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Ducted vs Direct Mount</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Sizing and CFM</a>
          <a href="#using-it" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. How to Use It</a>
          <a href="#humidity" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Climate Limits</a>
          <a href="#installation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Installation Notes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A whole house fan does something an air conditioner cannot: it replaces the entire volume of air in your house with cooler outside air, and it does it for a fraction of the electricity. The catch is that it only works when the outside air is genuinely cooler, and that it needs far more attic ventilation than most houses actually have.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1: How a Whole House Fan Actually Cools -->
        <div id="how-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How a Whole House Fan Actually Cools
          </h2>

          <p>
            A whole house fan does not cool air. It replaces it, which is a completely different mechanism from air conditioning and explains both its efficiency and its limits.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Exhausts Indoors Into the Attic</h3>

          <p>
            The fan sits in the ceiling and pulls air from the living space up into the attic, where it exits through roof and gable vents. That creates negative pressure inside, which draws outside air in through whatever windows you have opened.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Cooling Comes From the Outside Air</h3>

          <p>
            Nothing is chilled. If the outside air is twenty degrees cooler than inside, running the fan brings the house down towards that temperature in a matter of minutes. If the outside air is warmer, the fan makes the house hotter, which is why timing matters more than anything else.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Also Purges the Attic</h3>

          <p>
            A second and underrated benefit is that the attic itself gets flushed. An attic that has reached well over a hundred degrees during the day radiates that heat down through the ceiling all evening, and pushing cool air through it removes that reservoir.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Uses a Fraction of the Power</h3>

          <p>
            A whole house fan typically draws a few hundred watts where a central air conditioner draws several thousand. In a climate with cool evenings, running the fan for a few hours instead of air conditioning overnight is a large and very visible difference on a bill.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Is Not an Attic Fan</h3>

          <p>
            An attic fan ventilates only the attic and does nothing for the living space. A whole house fan moves air through the house itself. The two are constantly confused, and buying the wrong one is a common and disappointing mistake.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Cooling Maths</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
