        <!-- SECTION 2: Ducted vs Direct Mount -->
        <div id="ducted-vs-direct" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Ducted vs Direct Mount
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single biggest decision is whether the fan sits directly above the ceiling opening or remotely in the attic connected by a duct, and it determines how the machine feels to live with.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Direct mount puts the fan in the ceiling.</strong> Simple, cheap, compact, and effective. It also means every decibel the fan generates arrives in the room without anything to absorb it, which is why older whole house fans have a reputation for being deafening.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Ducted designs move the motor away.</strong> The fan body sits several feet away in the attic, connected by a flexible duct. The duct absorbs a substantial amount of the sound before it reaches the grille, and the result at ceiling level is dramatically quieter for the same airflow.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Ducted fans need attic clearance.</strong> The assembly is larger and needs room for the duct run, which rules it out in shallow roof spaces and low-pitch attics. Measure before ordering rather than after.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Dampers matter as much as the fan.</strong> Traditional louvres seal poorly and leak heated air all winter. Insulated damper boxes and motorised doors seal properly, and in a cold climate that difference can decide whether the fan saves money overall.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Multiple smaller fans beat one large one.</strong> Two moderate ducted fans in different parts of a house move air more evenly and more quietly than one very large fan in a hallway, and they allow cooling part of the house rather than all of it.
          </p>
        </div>

