        <!-- SECTION 4: Sizing a Fan to Your House -->
        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sizing a Fan to Your House
          </h2>

          <p>
            Undersizing produces a fan that runs all evening without noticeably changing the temperature. Oversizing produces one that cannot exhaust into the attic and chokes itself. Both are common.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Work from volume, not floor area.</strong> Multiply square footage by ceiling height to get cubic feet, then multiply by the air changes per hour you want and divide by sixty. Three air changes per hour is a reasonable minimum and six is a strong result.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Exclude the attic and the garage.</strong> Only count conditioned living space that the fan will actually draw air through. Including an unheated garage or basement inflates the requirement and pushes you into a fan larger than your venting can support.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Attic vent area is the real ceiling on size.</strong> The usual guidance is roughly one square foot of net free vent area for every 750 cubic feet per minute of fan capacity. A large fan in an under-vented attic simply cannot deliver its rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Net free area is not the same as vent size.</strong> A vent covered by screening and louvres passes far less air than its physical dimensions suggest, often less than half. Manufacturers publish net free area figures; use those rather than measuring the hole.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Two storey houses need more than the arithmetic suggests.</strong> Warm air collects upstairs, and a single fan in a downstairs hallway will not clear it effectively. Either place the fan on the upper floor or use two fans.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Check the Attic Vents Before You Buy the Fan</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Go into the attic and add up the net free area of every soffit, ridge, gable and roof vent. Multiply by 750 and that number is the largest fan your house can actually run. Buying a bigger one does not get you more air — it gets you a pressurised attic, a choked fan, and in some houses air pushed backwards through the soffits.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Running It Correctly -->
        <div id="using-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Running It Correctly
          </h2>

          <p>
            A whole house fan rewards being used deliberately, and the difference between good and poor operation is larger than the difference between most of the fans here.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Open the windows first, every time.</strong> The fan needs somewhere for replacement air to come from. Running it with the house closed pulls air down chimneys and through gas appliance flues, which can draw combustion products back into the house. This is the one genuine safety rule.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Open roughly four to six inches of window per thousand CFM.</strong> Too little opening starves the fan and increases noise as air rushes through gaps. Spread the openings across the rooms you want cooled rather than concentrating them in one place.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wait until outside is cooler than inside.</strong> Running it in the afternoon actively heats the house. A cheap indoor-outdoor thermometer is the single most useful accessory, and it usually shows the crossover happens later than people assume.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Start with a purge, then step down.</strong> Ten to fifteen minutes at high speed exchanges the air in the house and flushes the attic. After that, a lower speed maintains the flow through the night at much lower noise.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Close the windows in the morning.</strong> The entire strategy depends on capturing cool night air and then keeping it. Leaving windows open into the heat of the day undoes the work completely.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not run it with the air conditioning on.</strong> The fan exhausts the conditioned air you have paid to cool straight into the attic. They are alternatives, not complements, and the switchover point is when outdoor temperature drops below indoor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Where Whole House Fans Do Not Work -->
        <div id="humidity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Where Whole House Fans Do Not Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is one of the few home upgrades whose usefulness depends almost entirely on where you live, and honest advice has to include the places it does not suit.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Humid climates limit the benefit sharply.</strong> Pulling in air that is cooler but saturated makes the house feel clammy and loads moisture into the structure. Where summer nights stay humid, the temperature drop does not translate into comfort.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Dry climates with big day-night swings are ideal.</strong> A twenty five or thirty degree drop after sunset is exactly the condition a whole house fan exploits, and in those regions it can replace air conditioning for most of the season.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Allergy sufferers may struggle.</strong> The fan moves large volumes of unfiltered outdoor air through the house, pollen included. During a high pollen season that is a genuine reason not to run it, and no filtration option exists at these airflows.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wildfire smoke rules it out entirely.</strong> Drawing outdoor air in during a smoke event fills the house with particulate. The fan is exactly the wrong tool on those days, and it is worth having a plan that does not rely on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Very cold climates need the damper question answered first.</strong> Winter heat loss through a poorly sealing fan opening can exceed the summer saving. Either buy a fan with insulated motorised doors or commit to fitting an insulated cover each autumn.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Installation and Safety -->
        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Installation and Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Installation is within reach of a competent DIY installer, and the parts that go wrong are structural, electrical and ventilation rather than the fan itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not cut a ceiling joist without support.</strong> Most fans are sized to fit between standard joists for exactly this reason. If a larger fan requires cutting one, it needs proper headers, and that is a structural job rather than an afternoon.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Locate it centrally, usually in a hallway.</strong> A central position draws air evenly from the rooms around it. A fan at one end of the house pulls almost entirely from the nearest rooms and leaves the far end untouched.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Add attic venting before adding the fan.</strong> If the calculation says your venting is insufficient, additional gable or roof vents are cheaper than the fan and determine whether it performs. Doing this afterwards means living with a choked fan in the meantime.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Isolate the housing from the structure.</strong> Vibration transmitted into joists is heard throughout the house as a hum that has nothing to do with airflow. Rubber isolators or a proper suspension kit remove it, and most fans include one.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wire it properly, on its own circuit where required.</strong> Larger fans draw enough current to matter, and a timer switch is worth including so the fan does not run all night by accident. Local codes vary, and this is a reasonable point at which to involve an electrician.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Fit a winter cover if the damper is not insulated.</strong> A rigid insulated panel sitting over the ceiling opening from autumn to spring costs very little and stops the largest single heat loss the fan introduces.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Frequently Asked Questions -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
