              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Multiply your living area by ceiling height for the volume, choose three to six air changes per hour, and divide by sixty to get the CFM you need. Then check your attic venting: you need roughly one square foot of net free vent area per 750 CFM, and that is usually the real limit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I have to open windows?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, always, and this is a safety requirement rather than a performance tip. The fan exhausts air into the attic and something must replace it. With the house closed it can pull air down chimneys and gas appliance flues, drawing combustion products back inside.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it the same as an attic fan?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. An attic fan ventilates only the attic and does nothing for the temperature of your living space. A whole house fan pulls air through the house itself, exhausting into the attic and drawing cool outside air in through open windows. The two are frequently confused when shopping.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do they work in humid areas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Poorly. The fan brings in outdoor air unchanged, so if that air is cooler but saturated the house feels clammy and moisture is loaded into the structure. They work best in dry climates with a large drop in temperature between day and night, where the effect is dramatic.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much power does it use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically a few hundred watts, against several thousand for a central air conditioner. In a climate with cool evenings, running the fan for a few hours instead of air conditioning overnight produces a very visible reduction on a summer bill, which is the main reason people install them.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Whole House Fan Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most disappointment with whole house fans comes from installation and operation rather than the fan itself.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running It With the Windows Closed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The fan must have replacement air, and with the house sealed it will find it by pulling down chimneys and gas appliance flues, which can draw combustion products into the living space. Open several windows before switching it on, every single time, and spread them across the rooms you want cooled.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Attic Ventilation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A fan can only exhaust as fast as the attic can vent. Around one square foot of net free vent area per 750 CFM is the guidance, and most houses fall short. An oversized fan in an under-vented attic pressurises the space, runs well below its rating, and can push air back out through soffits.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running It During the Day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The fan brings in outside air unchanged, so running it when outdoors is hotter than indoors actively heats the house. Wait for the crossover in the evening, which an indoor-outdoor thermometer will show you happens later than most people assume.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving an Uninsulated Damper Uncovered in Winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Traditional louvres are thin metal flaps sealing by gravity, and they leak heated air into the attic every hour of the heating season. In a cold climate that loss can exceed the summer saving. Either buy a fan with insulated motorised doors or fit a rigid insulated cover each autumn.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

